# Benchmarking the AI Gateway (pre-Portal-integration)

Goal: before any Portal-side development starts, confirm the Gateway + on-prem
Ollama setup behaves reasonably under realistic concurrent load (target: 20
concurrent users). This is descriptive, not pass/fail — we're reading actual
latency/throughput/error-rate numbers, not checking against fixed thresholds.

Tool: `scripts/benchmark_gateway.py` in this repo. Simulates N concurrent
"users" calling the Gateway's OpenAI-compatible `/chat/completions` endpoint
for a fixed duration, then reports latency percentiles (p50/p95/p99), error
rate, and throughput, broken out per model.

## 1. Prerequisites (on whichever machine runs the test)

- Python 3.10+
- `pip install httpx`
- Network access to `https://ai-gateway.promon.co.in` (port 443 reachable from
  wherever you're running this — confirm you're not behind a firewall that
  blocks it before assuming a failure is the Gateway's fault)

## 2. Get the self-signed TLS cert

The Gateway uses a self-signed cert (not yet a publicly trusted CA — see
`tls_mode: selfsigned` in `inventories/production/group_vars/all.yml`), so the
script needs the CA file to verify the connection. It's the **public**
certificate, not a private key, so it's fine to send over Slack/email/etc.:

- File: `fetched-certs/ai-gateway.promon.co.in.crt` (in this repo, but
  gitignored — not something `git pull` will hand you, ask whoever ran the
  last `ansible-playbook site.yml` for a copy)

## 3. Get a scoped test API key — do NOT use the master key

Ask whoever holds `vault_litellm_master_key` to mint you a time-boxed,
budget-capped virtual key instead of handing out the master key:

```bash
curl -s --cacert fetched-certs/ai-gateway.promon.co.in.crt \
  https://ai-gateway.promon.co.in/key/generate \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer <master key>" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "duration": "7d",
    "max_budget": 5,
    "metadata": {"purpose": "pre-integration benchmark"}
  }'
```

The response JSON has a `"key"` field (e.g. `"key": "sk-tXL0wt5-lOOVK9sfY2UacA"`)
— that value is what goes into `--api-key` below. `max_budget` (USD) is a hard
spend cap enforced by LiteLLM itself, so even a runaway/misconfigured test run
can't overspend — this only matters once cloud models are involved (step 5).

## 4. Run the default benchmark — local model only, safe, no API cost

This is the one to run first. It only exercises `local-qwen25` (the on-prem
Ollama model), so there's no cloud API billing risk:

```bash
python3 scripts/benchmark_gateway.py \
  --base-url https://ai-gateway.promon.co.in \
  --api-key sk-... \
  --cacert fetched-certs/ai-gateway.promon.co.in.crt \
  --concurrency 20 \
  --duration 300
```

- `--concurrency 20` — simulates 20 concurrent users (our target)
- `--duration 300` — runs for 5 minutes; bump to e.g. `900` for a more
  representative sustained-load picture
- Each simulated user waits ~2s (randomized) between its own requests by
  default (`--think-time`), rather than hammering back-to-back — pass
  `--think-time 0` if you specifically want a pure burst/stress test instead
  of realistic usage

**Known thing to watch for**: `ollama_num_parallel: 1` in `all.yml` means the
local model processes exactly one request at a time internally — so with 20
concurrent users, expect requests to queue behind each other rather than run
in parallel. That's expected given current config, not a bug in the test.
Whether that's acceptable is exactly the kind of thing this benchmark should
surface — flag the resulting latency numbers back rather than assuming
something's broken.

## 5. Optional: include the cloud models — real money, get sign-off first

```bash
python3 scripts/benchmark_gateway.py \
  --base-url https://ai-gateway.promon.co.in \
  --api-key sk-... \
  --cacert fetched-certs/ai-gateway.promon.co.in.crt \
  --concurrency 20 \
  --duration 300 \
  --include-cloud
```

This also sends real, billed requests to `claude-general`/`gpt-general`/
`gemini-general` (Anthropic/OpenAI/Gemini) for the full duration. The script
prints a cost warning and pauses 3 seconds before starting so you can Ctrl+C
if this wasn't intended. **Get explicit go-ahead on this one before running
it** — a 20-concurrent-user, 5+ minute run against three paid APIs is a real
line item, not a rounding error, and the `max_budget` cap from step 3 is your
safety net if something runs longer than intended.

## 6. Reading the output

The script prints a per-model report:

```
--- local-qwen25 ---
  requests: 340   ok: 338   errors: 2   error_rate: 0.6%
  throughput: 1.13 req/s
  latency ms  mean=1820  p50=1650  p95=3400  p99=4100  max=5200
  cache signal: repeated-prompt p50=210ms vs unique-prompt p50=1900ms
```

- **p50/p95/p99** — median / 95th / 99th percentile latency in milliseconds.
  p95/p99 matter more than the mean for user-facing expectations — they show
  what your *slowest* real users actually experience, not just the average.
- **error_rate** — non-200 responses. Any error above ~0 is worth digging
  into via `docker logs promon-ai-litellm-gateway-1` — don't just note the
  number, find out *why*.
- **cache signal** — compares latency of intentionally-repeated prompts
  (which Valkey should cache) against unique ones. A large gap confirms the
  cache is doing something; a small/no gap is worth flagging.
- Raw per-request data lands in `benchmark_results.csv` (`--output` to
  change the path) for anyone who wants to plot distributions themselves.

## 7. Watch it live in Grafana while it runs

Since nothing's published to the host directly, reach Grafana via SSH local
port-forward while the benchmark is running, to correlate the CPU/memory
picture with the latency numbers as they happen rather than after the fact:

```bash
# on the VM, get the grafana container's internal IP
docker inspect -f '{{range .NetworkSettings.Networks}}{{.IPAddress}} {{end}}' promon-ai-grafana-1

# from your machine
ssh -L 3000:<that-ip>:3000 devops@169.58.97.147
# then browse http://localhost:3000
```

Specifically worth watching during the run: `ollama` container CPU (is it
pegged at its `resource_limits` cap while requests queue?) and whether
`litellm-gateway`/`valkey` show any memory growth over the run.

## 8. Report back

Send back: the printed report (or the CSV), plus what you observed in
Grafana during the run (was `ollama` CPU-bound? did latency degrade over the
5 minutes, or stay flat?). We're deciding whether this is ready for Portal
integration based on the actual shape of these numbers, not a fixed bar —
so the more concrete detail from the run, the better the call we can make.
